//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.mode, os.fs.create.mode, os.fs.symlink, os.fs.times, os.fs.truncate, os.fs.umask, os.fs.error, err.union
//!
//! s230 (wolf-lang#346, #698, #700, #702, #704) — the SHAPE rows of the
//! calls that change a file, deterministic on every host. Each call
//! judges its arguments before it asks the host anything: a mode outside
//! its bits (0o7777 for a change, 0o777 for a creation), an open mode
//! outside `[os.fs.open]`'s set, an empty link target, a nanosecond word
//! that is neither a count below a second nor -1 ("now") nor -2
//! ("leave"), a negative length, a mask outside 0o777 — each is
//! `invalid`, on windows too, where the well-formed call would be
//! `unsupported` instead. A forged handle is `io`. None of them reaches
//! the host, so `os_error()` stays 0 and nothing is created. The rows
//! that change something are `hard_link.lu`, `truncate.lu`,
//! `set_times.lu`, `mode_symlink.lu` and `corpus/os/umask.lu`.
## corpus/fs/hard_link.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="link=ok second_name_reads=more wolves\nagain=exists host_number=true\nmissing_source=not_found missing_parent=not_found\nafter_remove=more wolves one_name_left=true\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.link, os.fs.error, err.union
//!
//! s230 (wolf-lang#699, `[os.fs.link]`) — a hard link is a second name
//! of one file, on every tier-1 host. `fs_link(existing, path)` makes
//! `path` name the file `existing` names: bytes written through one
//! name are read through the other, and removing one name leaves the
//! file under the other (the link count, where the host answers one,
//! is back to 1). The call never replaces: a name that is taken is
//! `exists`, with the host's number beside the row — which is what
//! makes it the atomic publish `fs_rename` (which replaces) is not. A
//! source that is not there and a parent that is not there are both
//! `not_found`. The rows that need a second file system
//! (`cross_device`), a directory (`denied`) or a symbolic link (the
//! link itself is linked, never its target) are the unix witnesses in
//! `crates/wolf_driver/tests/fs_change_lanes.rs`.
## corpus/fs/mode_symlink.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="set_mode: true\nopen_perm: true\ncreate_dir_mode: true\nsymlink: true\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.mode, os.fs.create.mode, os.fs.symlink, os.fs.stat, os.fs.readlink, err.union
//!
//! s230 (wolf-lang#346, #698; `[os.fs.mode]`, `[os.fs.create.mode]`,
//! `[os.fs.symlink]`) — a mode set, a mode at creation and a symbolic
//! link, with one truth on every tier-1 host. Each is a host CAPABILITY:
//! linux, macOS and freebsd serve it, and windows answers `unsupported`
//! by name. So each line is asserted on both sides, in the program:
//!
//!   served        the effect is read back through `fs_stat` — the mode
//!                 is exactly the one set; a created file is 0o600 and a
//!                 created directory 0o700 from the first instant (the
//!                 owner's bits, which no sane umask takes); the link
//!                 reads back its target verbatim, is kind 3 to
//!                 `fs_lstat`, reads through to the file, refuses to
//!                 replace (`exists`), and a link to nothing is made
//!                 like any other;
//!   unsupported   nothing was made or changed, and the record does not
//!                 answer a mode either (`have` bit 4 clear).
## corpus/fs/set_times.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="both: atime=1000000000.123456700 mtime=1500000000.7654300 modified_ms=1500000000007\nleave: atime=1000000000.123456700 mtime=1600000000.0\nnow: atime_kept=true mtime_is_now=true\ndirectory: mtime=1400000000\nbefore_epoch: atime=-1.999999900 mtime=-86400.0\nmissing=not_found host_number=true\ncleaned=true")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.times, os.fs.stat, os.fs.error, err.union
//!
//! s230 (wolf-lang#700, `[os.fs.times]`) — a file's access and
//! modification times, set and read back through `fs_stat`'s words
//! 12..15, on every tier-1 host. `fs_set_times(path, atime_s, atime_ns,
//! mtime_s, mtime_ns, follow)` takes each time as whole seconds from the
//! Unix epoch (negative before it, floored) and nanoseconds, or — in the
//! nanosecond word — one of two special values: **-1**, the host's clock
//! now, and **-2**, leave this time as it is (the seconds beside either
//! are not read). So one call sets both, sets one and keeps the other
//! with no read in between, or stamps "now". The nanoseconds here are
//! multiples of 100 because windows keeps a time in 100 ns steps. A
//! directory's times are set like a file's; a path that is not there is
//! `not_found`. A link's own times (`follow` false on a link) are the
//! unix witness in `crates/wolf_driver/tests/fs_change_lanes.rs`.
## corpus/fs/truncate.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="shorter=4 offset=10\nlonger=8 kept=51 zeros=0,0\nwrite_at_offset=11 hole=0\nempty=0\nclosed=io host_number=0\nread_only=io host_number=true size_kept=12")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.fs.truncate, os.fs.error, err.union
//!
//! s230 (wolf-lang#702, `[os.fs.truncate]`) — a file's length, set on a
//! handle, on every tier-1 host. `fs_truncate(fd, len)` makes the file
//! exactly `len` bytes: shorter (the tail is gone), or longer with zeros
//! no write stored. The handle's offset does NOT move — after ten bytes
//! written and a truncation to four the offset is still 10, and the next
//! write lands there, leaving zeros between. Every host refusal is `io`:
//! a closed handle (no host number: the implementation decided it) and a
//! handle opened for reading (the host's number beside the row; the file
//! keeps its length — 12, the one byte written at the unmoved offset 11
//! after the truncation to nothing). A negative length is `invalid` before any of that
//! (`change_rows.lu`).
