//!
//! s225 (`[os.env.unset]`; wolf-lang#534) — `env_unset` removes a
//! variable: `env_get` answers `missing` after it. Removing a variable
//! that is not there succeeds (POSIX `unsetenv`), and the names
//! `env_set` refuses (`=`, empty) are `invalid` here too. A later `env_set` brings the name back. Every
//! machine runs it: the checked machine and lupin over their overlays,
//! native and release over the program's own environment. That a CHILD
//! no longer sees an unset variable is a spawn, so it is the unix
//! witness in `crates/wolf_driver/tests/exec_lanes.rs`. That `env_vars`
//! no longer lists it is pinned beside the checked machine's and the
//! runtime's own tests, not here: lupin has no `env_vars` (its
//! environment is hermetic, s40), so a row that lists would part on it
//! for a reason that is not this one.
## corpus/os/exec_rows.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="no_argv=invalid\nno_equals=invalid\nempty_name=invalid\nodd_map=invalid\nout_of_range=invalid\nrepeated=invalid\nbad_source=invalid\nstill_here")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: os.proc.exec, err.union
//!
//! s225 (`[os.proc.exec]`; wolf-lang#534) — the exec rows that replace
//! nothing, deterministic on every host. `os_exec(exe, argv, env, map)`
//! decides its SHAPE before anything else is read: an empty argv (argv[0]
//! is the new program's own name and must be given), an environment entry
//! with no `=` or an empty name, and every map that `os_spawn_fds` would
//! call `invalid` are each `invalid` — on windows too, where a well-formed
